  "worked_solution": "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-6x-3 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-6x & =3 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}x] & =3 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}] & =3 \\\\ 4[{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{16}] & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} & =3 \\\\ 4{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =3 \\\\ 4{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =3+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 4{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{3}{4}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{4} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{16} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{16}} \\\\ |x-\\frac{3}{4}| & =\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{16}} \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{4} & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{21}{16}} \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{4} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{4}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{21}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
